  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and thunderstorms move east-southeast from \nNY after midnight and overnight mainly for areas south of the Mass \nPike. Turning more humid too.\n\nSeasonably mild with tolerable humidity levels currently as of \nWednesday afternoon, but we're looking at some changes to our \nairmass over the next 12-24 hours. Couple weak impulses in broad WNW \nflow aloft are contributing to scattered showers with a few \nthunderstorms. One is working through far northeast Ontario, \nresponsible for the area of showers and storms over VT/northern NY. \nThese should pass to our north but a couple could clip part of Essex \nCounty and the North Shore through sundown. \n\nThe more significant feature to Southern New England comes in the \nform of a weak impulse on water vapor imagery near the northern \nportion of Lake Michigan, which is currently helping to produce \nscattered showers and storms when coupled with isentropic ascent \nassociated with a surface warm front/higher dewpoints in the mid 60s \nover Greater Toronto and Buffalo NY areas. NBM and CAMs were \npreviously slow to resolve these, but the past few cycles have shown \nmore of a risk for showers and storms more concentrated along and \nsouth of the Mass Pike and particularly for the Hartford-Providence-\nsoutheast MA area around/after midnight and into overnight. Mid-\nlevel lapse rates around 6 to 6.5 C/km but with dewpoints rising \ninto the mid 60s, most-unstable CAPE values rise to about 1000-1200 \nJ/kg in the CT-RI area. Expect a few thunderstorms to be around but \nshould be elevated, with a couple stronger storms capable of \nfrequent lightning, pea to dime size hail and wind gusts around 40 \nmph as they move off toward the southeast at a fairly good clip \ngiven fast WNW winds aloft. Not thinking storms rise to the level of \nsevere weather. Otherwise, will turn a bit more humid than it's been \nbut not oppressive, with lows in the mid to upper 60s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Early cloud cover Thursday dissipates, but isolated \nto widely scattered showers/weaker storms develop across northern MA.\n\nShowers/embedded storms from overnight should be peeling offshore \nearly Thursday morning, with considerable cloudiness around during \nthe morning hours. Gradual heating and mixing should allow for cloud \ncover to scatter out. While most of the day should be more-or-less \ndry, there is a risk for mid-afternoon showers or weak thunderstorms \nmainly north of the Mass Pike as a stronger shortwave trough aloft \nmoves from northern Quebec into ME/NH and swings a cold front \nsouthward. I think the coverage and strength of showers or weak \nstorms could be limited by drier air aloft, to go along with delayed \nheating/destabilization, so have capped PoPs to no more than 30%. So \nwhile Thursday ends up dry, there should be some showers or weak \nstorms to dodge Thursday afternoon through sundown, but nothing that \nlooks hazardous. Will begin to turn less humid late upon cold \nfrontal passage.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Becoming cooler and less humid Friday through \nSaturday night. A period of showers and possible thunderstorms \nSunday afternoon into Monday Night. Dry weather should return \nTuesday and Wednesday.\n\nGaining some clarity that a cold front should be over the coastal \nwaters friday morning and moving farther offshore during the day. \nHigh pressure then settles across southern New England for Saturday, \nbefore it too moves offshore Sunday. The main impact will be drier \nand cooler conditions during this time.\n\nAnticipating a brief change back to more humid conditions late \nSunday into Monday night. This should be when a low pressure passes \nby to our north, but drags its associated fronts through southern \nNew England. The combination of the increased humidity and the lift \nfrom some front means at least a period of showers. Not overly \nconcerned about thunderstorms just yet, but will need to keep this \nin mind with later forecasts. This should not be a washout this \nentire time, but the greatest risk for showers looks to be Sunday \nnight. \n\nA large high pressure over the Midwest should start nudging into our \nregion Tuesday and be more of a factor in our weather during the \nmiddle of next week.",
